\chapter{Distributed Orchestration}

\section{Overview}

The AAAHSC--9.4 architecture is designed for sovereign-grade distributed
execution across heterogeneous GPU clusters. This chapter describes the
multi-node orchestration layer combining:

\begin{itemize}
    \item MPI domain decomposition for numerical partitioning,
    \item Kubernetes pod scheduling for elastic deployment,
    \item GPU affinity mapping for AMR block placement,
    \item non-blocking communication for real-time evolution.
\end{itemize}

The orchestration layer transforms AAAHSC--9.4 from a single-node solver
into a scalable scientific instrument capable of exascale throughput.

\section{MPI Domain Decomposition}

The computational domain is decomposed into AMR blocks:
\begin{equation}
    \Omega = \bigcup_{b=1}^{N_{\text{blocks}}} \Omega_b,
\end{equation}
where each block $\Omega_b$ is assigned to an MPI rank.

MPI ranks are arranged in a Cartesian topology:
\begin{equation}
    \text{MPI\_Cart\_create}(P_x, P_y, P_z),
\end{equation}
ensuring locality of communication.

Each rank stores:
\begin{itemize}
    \item BSSN variables,
    \item gauge fields,
    \item curvature invariants,
    \item constraint buffers,
    \item AMR metadata.
\end{itemize}

\section{Non-Blocking Communication}

Inter-block synchronization uses non-blocking MPI primitives:
\begin{align}
    \text{MPI\_Isend}(Q_b, \text{dest}), \\
    \text{MPI\_Irecv}(Q_b, \text{src}),
\end{align}
followed by:
\begin{equation}
    \text{MPI\_Waitall}.
\end{equation}

This ensures:
\begin{itemize}
    \item overlap of communication and computation,
    \item minimal latency,
    \item scalability across thousands of ranks.
\end{itemize}

\section{GPU Affinity Placement}

Each AMR block is mapped to a GPU using:
\begin{equation}
    \text{CUDA\_SetDevice}(\text{rank} \mod N_{\text{GPU}}).
\end{equation}

This ensures:
\begin{itemize}
    \item deterministic GPU assignment,
    \item balanced load distribution,
    \item minimal PCIe/NVLink contention.
\end{itemize}

Blocks with higher curvature are preferentially placed on GPUs with
greater compute capability.

\section{Kubernetes Pod Scheduling}

AAAHSC--9.4 is deployed as a Kubernetes workload using:

\begin{itemize}
    \item StatefulSets for deterministic rank identity,
    \item DaemonSets for node-level GPU provisioning,
    \item ConfigMaps for simulation parameters,
    \item Secrets for PQC key storage.
\end{itemize}

Each MPI rank runs inside a Kubernetes pod. Pod scheduling ensures:

\begin{itemize}
    \item node-level GPU availability,
    \item anti-affinity rules for high-load blocks,
    \item automatic rescheduling on node failure,
    \item elastic scaling based on AMR refinement.
\end{itemize}

\section{Elastic Scaling}

The AMR hierarchy evolves dynamically. When refinement increases the
number of blocks:
\begin{equation}
    N_{\text{blocks}}(t+\Delta t) > N_{\text{blocks}}(t),
\end{equation}
Kubernetes automatically launches additional pods.

MPI ranks are reassigned using:
\begin{equation}
    \text{MPI\_Comm\_split}.
\end{equation}

This enables:
\begin{itemize}
    \item real-time scaling,
    \item adaptive resource allocation,
    \item efficient handling of strong-field events.
\end{itemize}

\section{Fault Tolerance}

If a node fails:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Kubernetes reschedules the affected pod,
    \item MPI performs communicator repair,
    \item AMR metadata is restored from checkpoint,
    \item evolution resumes without global restart.
\end{enumerate}

Checkpointing uses:
\begin{equation}
    Q_b(t) \rightarrow \text{Checkpoint}(b,t),
\end{equation}
stored in distributed object storage.

\section{Topology Summary}

The distributed orchestration layer provides:

\begin{itemize}
    \item MPI-based numerical partitioning,
    \item GPU-native AMR block placement,
    \item Kubernetes-driven elasticity,
    \item PQC-secured telemetry integration,
    \item fault-tolerant multi-node execution.
\end{itemize}

This transforms AAAHSC--9.4 into a cloud-native relativistic simulation
instrument capable of real-time, observationally anchored gravitational
modeling.